   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the election law, in relation to lever voting machines",
    "basePrintNo": "S7370",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7370-2013",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §15-114, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S7370\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, in relation to lever\nvoting machines\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure the security of lever voting machines used in village\nelections after such elections.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\n§ 1. The election law is amended by adding a new subdivision 2 to\nsection 15-114 and existing subdivision 2 is renumbered as subdivision\n3. This new section states that lever voting machines used in village\nelections shall remain locked against voting for a period of 30 days or\nuntil 15 days before the next election, if such machines are needed for\nuse at the next election. It also specifies the conditions under which\nthe machines may be unlocked.\n\n§ 3. This act shall take effect immediately and be deemed repealed on\nDecember 31, 2014.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nBill A.7453/S.5385 (passed Senate June 14, 2011) removed language from\nthe election law relating to the post-election security and disclosure\nof the contents of lever voting machines and updated the election law to\nprovide for the security and storage of electronic memory devices as\nused by the new voting system. However, some villages throughout New\nYork State may, until December 31, 2014, use lever voting machines for\ntheir local elections. This bill inserts the lever-machine security and\ndisclosure measures taken out by S.5385 and applies them to villages\nusing the lever machines. The provisions of this bill expire on December\n31, 2014.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2012: A8088 passed assembly\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately and be deemed repealed on Decem-\nber 31, 2014.\n\n",
